re  thinking about in business and in some  NGOs that work with business I'm not  sure I readable this is but in T we  tried to come up with some rough  estimates of what are these different  markets how big are they now how big  could they become over the next 20 or 40  years and this comes from forests trends  which keeps and the ecosystem  marketplace which you may have seen it's  a interesting website they keep tabs on  these different markets and try to to  get a sense of what what's going on in  them and how quickly they're developing  you can see from this that certified  agricultural products are currently the  biggest and are expected to remain the  biggest area and I think that's maybe a  testament to the success of those  labeling and certification schemes but  you can also see projections of rapid  growth in some other areas just to  highlight here for example bio carbon in  red this actually could be well above 10  billion dollars a year but those were  the projections at the time and  certified forest products again expected  to increase dramatically even though  it's already a big part of the of the  the forest sector those are hypothetical  guesses at best for a range of  initiatives which sometimes are called  payments for ecosystem services I'm sure  you've heard about these and you  probably are familiar if you haven't  memorized the classic definition of  payment for ecosystem services put out  by span wonder  a little over five years ago a voluntary  transaction that's very important like  any market transaction has to be a  willing buyer and a willing seller for a  well-defined environmental service again  very important what exactly are you  buying and selling or a land-use likely  to secure that service which is a bit of  a get out clause if you can't quantify  the service itself you try to define the  type of land management practices or  marine ecosystem management practices  that would generate the services that  you're interested in you need at least  one buyer at least one seller and  conditionality is critical you don't get  paid unless you provide the service I  mean this is all sounds like any market  but from an environmental perspective it  was considered quite radical when the  concept was was put forward what does it  look like in practice this is from  Stefan padula at the World Bank he's  again been a leading thinker in what our  payments for ecosystem services and how  to implement them he's been doing it all  over the world for several years we have  this standard problem conventional  management of a resource generates a  certain level of benefit but imposes  external costs that the producer ignores  more sustainable management of that same  resource in the current market generates  less value and therefore it's not  favored the payment for ecosystem  service scheme simply says well if we  pay a supplement on top of the  conservation management what what is  already earned by conservation  management we can get resource users to  shift from the conventional to the  conservation management regime and  there's this trade-off between how much  or how little you need to pay the  minimum payment is the opportunity cost  to the resource manager of making that  shift from conventional to conservation  management the maximum payment is the  willingness to pay to avoid those  damages and that's where the negotiation  and issues of market power come in as to  who's going to get that that surplus I'm  not going to talk about the catskills  case because you've already heard about  it but just to mention  that although everybody talks about the  Catskills there are many many other  examples this is from France just near  where I was living and still living for  the next few weeks if y'all is a brand  you're probably familiar with they  discovered many years ago that there was  a threat to their water supply they  didn't call it payment for ecosystem  services they just realized that if they  wanted to protect the value of their  asset and their license to bottled water  they needed to deal with the threat the  threat was agricultural runoff and not  surprisingly they got in touch with the  local farmers union and with local  governments and came to a deal and I  don't know what price they ended up  agreeing but they came to a bilateral  deal with the the communities in the  upper watershed that supplies their  resource and it's a complicated chart  but it because it basically shows all  the different parties that need to be  involved in this this deal but another  example would be Perry AV tell another  well-known water brand they've also done  similar initiatives with farmers in  another part of France and you can find  examples in Costa Rica of a brewery that  have done similar deals so this is not  just the catskills although people like  to use that example because it's it's  relatively well documented okay one  question that people often ask about  payments for ecosystem services is what  does this mean for poverty reduction  particularly in developing countries on  the plus side there might be new sources  of income you might be able to diversify  your income and thereby reduce risks so  you're not just dependent on crop prices  or commodity prices but you have a  separate source of income that is  uncorrelated with those there might be  put opportunities to strengthen local  institutions as people start organizing  themselves around payment schemes and  learning how to manage resources in a  way that delivers ecosystem services  contributes to human capital formation  that's all fine but then on the  downside you can't sell what you don't  own and so if resource tenure is an  issue payments for ecosystem services  might be difficult to set up or those  people who don't have legal title may  not be able to participate the setup  costs can be quite substantial we've  seen that in many examples the capacity  of governments to monitor and enforce  contracts and sure that services are  being delivered and that people are  being paid for those services is not  always their corruption could be a major  issue but I want to highlight in  particular the issue of reduced access  to resources and reduce labor demand and  we've seen this even in the United  States they've been some interesting  studies looking at the Conservation  Reserve Program which is managed by the  US Department of Agriculture if you pay  farmers to take land out of production  to deliver ecosystem benefits or  conservation benefits you only have to  pay the farmer enough to make them  better off but remember the farmer is  looking at their net profits so you  don't have to pay for all the things  that a farmer has to do to generate  those net profits right farmers by seed  fertilizer machinery and all of those  things are provided by other people in  the local economy so if you pay the  farmer their net profits they're happy  and you get your conservation benefit  but the seed supplier the fertilizer  supplier the agricultural equipment  supplier and the person who maintains  the tractors they're out of a job and we  see that in other countries as well that  payments for ecosystem services can  deliver income to resource owners but  they can also undermine the rest of the  rural economy which is predicated on  certain production systems so you need  to think about how is the rest of the  rural economy affected by these these  new initiatives and I come back to the  Madagascar case where we have  potentially a very large carbon benefit  and potentially a significant  opportunity cost  loss of access to resources loss of  agricultural production and the obvious  response to that which you will have  thought of already is let's use the  carbon value to compensate for the  opportunity cost and so one of the  things that this company is looking at  now is how to capture that carbon value  through a red project how to set up a  scheme that would channel at least part  of that revenue back to the local  communities who are sacrificing their  access to this forest for the sake of  biodiversity it can work because in this  case the values in theory make that  possible whether it will work in  practice really depends as I said on a  lot of institutional factors the extent  of corruption which in Madagascar is  sometimes an issue and whether the  company is really is going to keep to  its word to to make this happen so  that's what I had prepared for today I  realize we've got about ten minutes left  and I'm happy to go back to any slides  or take any comments or questions I'll  start there
 

